\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Illustration of the four simulation variants. Two aspects of the reconstruction are varied: the projected contour size, using either the original image-derived contours or a first-frame body profile fitted along the reconstructed spine, and the boundary forcing, i.e. which components of the boundary-point velocity are imposed on the surrounding fluid ($y$ only, or both $x$ and $y$). This results in four variants: Raw (original contours, $y$-only forcing), Size-constrained (fitted profile, $y$-only forcing), X-deformation (original contours, two-component forcing) and Full (fitted profile, two-component forcing). The shapes shown are conceptual illustrations of the projected contours and do not represent the actual simulation results.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:models}{{2}{4}{Illustration of the four simulation variants. Two aspects of the reconstruction are varied: the projected contour size, using either the original image-derived contours or a first-frame body profile fitted along the reconstructed spine, and the boundary forcing, i.e. which components of the boundary-point velocity are imposed on the surrounding fluid ($y$ only, or both $x$ and $y$). This results in four variants: Raw (original contours, $y$-only forcing), Size-constrained (fitted profile, $y$-only forcing), X-deformation (original contours, two-component forcing) and Full (fitted profile, two-component forcing). The shapes shown are conceptual illustrations of the projected contours and do not represent the actual simulation results}{figure.2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:models@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][4][]4}}
